  "requirement": "`habeas_sdk::graph` MUST define captured dependencies as follows:\n\n```rust,ignore\n#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]\npub enum Dependency<N> {\n    Node(N, Fingerprint),\n    OutgoingEdges(N, Fingerprint),\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject `requirements.sdk.graph.evidence.dependencies` only requires that `habeas_sdk::graph` define the public enum `Dependency<N>`. The enum has two variants, `Node(N, Fingerprint)` and `OutgoingEdges(N, Fingerprint)`. It derives `Clone`, `Debug`, `PartialEq` and `Eq`.\n\nThe only implementation site is `crates/habeas-sdk/src/graph.rs#enum[Dependency]`. It is a pure type declaration with no conditionals or transformations, so no separate behavioral test is needed. The compiler enforces the variant names, their field shapes and the derives. The implementation also derives `Serialize` and `Deserialize`. The requirement doesn't forbid that.\n\nNo test carries a `{test}` reference to this requirement. The existing tests still use the type in the ways the requirement describes:\n- Tests in `tests/validation.rs` build both variants and compare `Vec<Dependency<_>>` values with `assert_eq!`. Examples are `snapshots_record_exact_reads_and_retain_distinct_versions`, which keeps two `Node` entries for the same node with different fingerprints, and `explicit_insertions_preserve_order_repetition_and_selection_snapshots`, which mixes `OutgoingEdges` and `Node`. This exercises `PartialEq` and `Debug`.\n- `tests/agent.rs` pattern-matches `Dependency::Node(node, fingerprint)` and `Dependency::OutgoingEdges(node, fingerprint)`. This checks the field shapes, with a node and a `Fingerprint`.\n\nThis is enough for a declaration with no logic. I found no untested obligation and no acknowledged gap.",
